 "prompt": "The repository at /testbed has a bug, described below. Fix it by changing the source code; do not modify or\nadd tests. When you are done, the project's existing test suite should pass.\n\n<issue>\n# OAuth2 Metadata Endpoint returns incorrect content type and status code\n\nI've discovered an issue with the OAuth2 Metadata Endpoint. The endpoint is returning XML content type instead of JSON and a 500 status code instead of 200.\n\nHere's a reproduction of the issue:\n\n```python\nfrom oauthlib.oauth2.rfc6749.endpoints import TokenEndpoint, MetadataEndpoint\n\n# Create a simple token endpoint\nendpoint = TokenEndpoint(None, None, grant_types={'password': None})\n\n# Create metadata endpoint with required fields\nmetadata = MetadataEndpoint([endpoint], {\n    'issuer': 'https://foo.bar', \n    'token_endpoint': 'https://foo.bar/token'\n})\n\n# Get the response\nheaders, body, status = metadata.create_metadata_response('/', 'GET')\n\n# Check the headers and status\nprint(f\"Content-Type: {headers['Content-Type']}\")  # Shows application/xml instead of application/json\nprint(f\"Status: {status}\")  # Shows 500 instead of 200\n```\n\nAdditionally, there seem to be issues with the validation logic in the metadata endpoint. For example:\n\n1. The Access-Control-Allow-Origin header is set to 'localhost' instead of '*'\n2. The validation logic for required fields appears to be inverted\n3. The validation for URLs and lists seems to be checking the opposite of what it should\n\nThis is causing problems when trying to use the metadata endpoint for OAuth2 server implementations, as clients expect proper JSON responses with the correct status codes and validation.\n</issue>\n",
